{"success": true, "name": "arc-grid-solving-continual", "file": "references/diff-mask-lesson.md", "content": "# Exact diff masks for motif/propagation hypotheses

A conspicuous seed motif does not prove that every translated match should be painted. For each demonstration, compute the complete input/output diff as a set of coordinates and classify each changed cell by its original value. Independently enumerate predicted changes for candidate mechanisms (mask translations, rays, periodic propagation, or other spatial rules), including any eligibility condition such as requiring a particular background value.

Validate exact set equality: every changed demo cell must be predicted, and every predicted cell must actually change. Record unchanged lookalikes and existing motif cells as negative evidence. Keep generator, eligible input values, and selection scope separate; after an incorrect submission reject the complete generator rather than making a cosmetic adjustment. Apply the surviving rule to the test only after it fits all demos. This is especially important when demos vary in palette, seed geometry, or grid dimensions.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-hermes-luna6-low-seq0/frozen/held-out/play/users/u081/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-grid-solving-continual/references/diff-mask-lesson.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "continual-arc-demo-checkpoint", "file": "references/sparse-geometry-vs-template.md", "content": "# Sparse geometry versus motif copying

When a sparse grid contains a compact colored motif plus isolated same-color marks, the motif may cue a geometric operation rather than serve as a template to replicate. Record the exact motif anchor and every marker coordinate. Test whether aligned markers define endpoints/borders and whether intervening cells are completed; infer eligible orientation, endpoint class, and scope separately. Account for existing markers that remain unchanged. Compare this with literal template-occurrence predictions against every demo, including negative evidence, before acting. A varied second geometry can discriminate the candidate mechanisms; request it only when it could alter the prediction.
