{"success": true, "name": "continual-arc-mixed-demo-review", "file": "references/cellwise-mask-and-generator-validation.md", "content": "# Cellwise mask validation across heterogeneous demos

When an ARC task has a distinctive colored seed in noisy grids, do not equate resemblance with the rule. Track the seed shape, eligible target values, and spatial generator as independent hypotheses. For every demo, enumerate the full input/output difference mask and preserve negative evidence: unchanged lookalikes, untouched target-valued cells, and the original seed.

Before another demo or a submission, replay the proposed generator against every pair, especially when seed shape, palette, or dimensions vary. A literal translated-template matcher that looks plausible on one example but fails the varied demos is falsified; do not patch it by shifting a few cells. Request additional examples only for explicit remaining alternatives and a specific discriminating observation.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-hermes-luna6-low-seq0/frozen/held-out/play/users/u081/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/continual-arc-mixed-demo-review/references/cellwise-mask-and-generator-validation.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "continual-arc-demo-checkpoint", "file": "references/separator-vs-motif-check.md", "content": "# Full-width separator versus salient interior motif

When a test grid has a complete horizontal/vertical line alongside a conspicuous patterned region, don't select a transform just because the motif is visually rich. Start from exact input/output diffs in every demo. Record separator coordinates and whether separator cells change, plus every changed isolated marker and unchanged lookalikes. Compare candidate rules that operate on the separator/bands against rules operating on the motif, and require a single candidate to explain all positive and negative cells before projecting to the test.

In this instance, a minimal demo changed a sparse marker and recolored the adjacent full-width row below a full-width divider; another recolored the row immediately above a full-width divider while preserving a lone marker. The test had a bottom full-width color-2 row and an unrelated dense color-1 patch. The initial guess recolored the row above the divider and copied the patch as if it were a template; it was rejected. Do not infer a color-8 band merely by mirroring the first demo's local row offset. Need more discriminating evidence to establish which band relative to divider is selected and what sparse markers indicate.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-hermes-luna6-low-seq0/frozen/held-out/play/users/u081/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/continual-arc-demo-checkpoint/references/separator-vs-motif-check.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "continual-arc-demo-integration-discipline", "file": "references/curator-review-lesson.md", "content": "# Curator review lesson: make the demo checkpoint operational

A repeated failure mode is issuing successive demo requests on heterogeneous Continual-ARC pairs without integrating each response, then making an unsupported test guess. A generic instruction to "identify competing hypotheses" is too easy to skip. Before any next request, the solver should have explicitly compared exact diffs (including identity/no-op and unchanged structures), stated the strongest cross-demo relation and unresolved alternatives, and named a concrete observation that a new example could distinguish. If that comparison cannot be made, stop requesting and synthesize the current evidence first.

Postmortem references should not become proxy evidence about the current task. Load one only to answer a specific identified question, then return to the actual demo grids and verify the rule cell-by-cell. A previous session's outcome is a cautionary workflow note, not proof of this task's transformation.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-hermes-luna6-low-seq0/frozen/held-out/play/users/u081/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/continual-arc-demo-integration-discipline/references/curator-review-lesson.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "continual-arc-error-recovery", "file": "references/local-demo-global-rule.md", "content": "# Separating a demo's local effect from its global rule

A minimal example may reveal what happens to particular cells without proving how those cells are selected in larger grids. In this session, the 2×10 pair established that a trailing run of 9s becomes 8s and that part of a run remains unchanged, but it did not validate a whole-grid operation for a separate 22×25 test. The first submission simply erased the test's 1-cells; rejection falsified that extrapolation. The next submission erased all test marks except an isolated vertical pair, without a demonstration supporting that selection, and was also unsupported.

When demo and test scales differ sharply, separate and validate: (1) local value mapping, (2) trigger/selection, (3) propagation or geometry, and (4) scope. Keep unchanged demo cells as constraints. If multiple global generators still fit, request a demo designed to distinguish them rather than submitting a conjecture. Never infer an exception mask solely from a visually salient test pattern.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-hermes-luna6-low-seq0/frozen/held-out/play/users/u081/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/continual-arc-error-recovery/references/local-demo-global-rule.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "continual-arc-error-recovery", "file": "references/trajectory-after-error.md", "content": "# Revising sparse trajectory hypotheses after a wrong output

A wrong submission that extends only one apparent ray is not repaired by shifting its start or changing a single cell. Re-derive the generator from each demo by recording every seed marker, color, direction vector, and endpoint. In the example reviewed here, one demo was identity (a sparse object did not trigger extension), while the larger demo showed simultaneous rays: a 7-ray extended down-right from its marker, and 6-ray(s) extended along the other diagonal direction from multiple marker locations. The test had several 9 markers plus paired 7 and 6 markers; assuming the salient 9-marker cluster was irrelevant and extending just one 6 ray / one short 7 ray was unsupported and was rejected. General lesson: compare the full input geometry to positive and negative demos; infer whether multiple marker colors or every compatible seed launches a ray, and project each ray through the full grid to the evidenced boundary. Preserve original objects and background. Do not submit a one-color or one-ray interpretation unless it accounts for all trajectory behavior in the demonstrations.

This example is not a universal rule for launching rays from all markers: use it to formulate competing hypotheses, then select the one that explains the complete demos and test configuration.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-hermes-luna6-low-seq0/frozen/held-out/play/users/u081/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/continual-arc-error-recovery/references/trajectory-after-error.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "continual-arc-demo-checkpoint", "file": "references/post-error-fill-scope-review.md", "content": "# Post-error fill scope review

This stream contained one demo in which gaps between aligned markers were colored, another where the same kind of candidate rule was not evident, an identity example featuring a closed rectangular outline, and a test with several tempting but structurally different boundary patterns. Two speculative fill submissions failed. The durable lesson is to separate (1) whether the geometry is aligned/enclosed, (2) what configuration triggers action, and (3) which exact cells are in scope. An identity example is strong negative evidence against indiscriminately filling every enclosure or same-row/column gap.

After a rejected fill, reconstruct all demo diffs and explicitly compare gap-filling, enclosure-filling, and identity-preservation hypotheses against every pair. Do not infer the correct mask from a prior session note; verify the current grid's boundaries, coordinates, and dimensions. Spend another demo only after naming competing hypotheses and the specific outcome that would distinguish them. Treat a previous session's noted correction as a candidate to verify, never as a substitute for current-instance evidence.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-hermes-luna6-low-seq0/frozen/held-out/play/users/u081/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/continual-arc-demo-checkpoint/references/post-error-fill-scope-review.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "continual-arc-demo-checkpoint", "file": "references/session-consecutive-demo-synthesis.md", "content": "# Consecutive demo requests without synthesis

In this instance, five demo requests were made in succession across turns, despite each new pair changing the evidence materially. The examples looked unlike each other locally, but their salient relation was a long straight divider/marker line in color 2 plus an object on one side. In demonstrations, the output added a parallel line of color 8 on the opposite side of the object/partition, preserving the original line and object. The test similarly has a vertical 2 line at column 14 and a compact 0 motif immediately to its left; this suggests completing a parallel 8 line at column 11. This is the kind of structural synthesis that should happen after each pair, not after exhausting several demos.

Operational lesson: after every returned demo, record divider orientation/location, side containing the object, output line orientation/location, and preserved cells. Compare candidate relations across all prior examples. Do not issue the next request unless a specific unresolved fork remains and a new pair could distinguish it. A repeated pattern of asking for demos without stating the evolving hypotheses spends budget without increasing useful evidence.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-hermes-luna6-low-seq0/frozen/held-out/play/users/u081/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/continual-arc-demo-checkpoint/references/session-consecutive-demo-synthesis.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "continual-arc-error-recovery", "file": "references/rejected-hypothesis-checkpoint.md", "content": "# Rejection checkpoint: fully re-derive before resubmitting

After an incorrect answer, reject the generator—not just its final values. In divider-separated/panelized inputs, re-establish the panel boundaries and exact input-to-output coordinate mapping, then determine what output cells encode. A pointwise comparison across panels can seem compelling but still yield false positives or miss required outputs. Replay it over every position in the full demo, including all output zeros, before transferring it to the test. If the relation is not validated, do not submit a cosmetic variation; request a demonstration only when it distinguishes named competing hypotheses.
