{"success": true, "name": "continual-arc-mixed-demo-review", "file": "references/seed-motif-generator-checkpoint.md", "content": "# Seed-motif generator checkpoint after a wrong submission

A salient colored seed embedded in a noisy grid does not by itself imply a copy rule. Candidate classes can include translated shape occurrences, periodic/lattice replication, directional rays, or combinations. Before extrapolating, record the seed's exact coordinates and the complete input/output difference mask in every demo. Verify both positive and negative cases: why each changed cell is selected, and why visually similar unchanged cells are excluded. Check palettes and dimensions that vary across demos; distinguish the seed color, target/background color, and unrelated noise empirically rather than assuming one role from a single pair.

After an incorrect submission, discard the whole generator. Do not move a few cells, swap a presumed background color, or switch to a nearby geometric interpretation without replaying it against every demo. With a costly attempt budget, use remaining demos for explicit competing hypotheses and only submit after a full-mask validation. Preserve all cells outside the supported transformation and verify dimensions/row lengths.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-hermes-luna6-low-seq0/frozen/held-out/play/users/u081/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/continual-arc-mixed-demo-review/references/seed-motif-generator-checkpoint.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "continual-arc-demo-integration", "file": "references/demo-chain-pitfall.md", "content": "# Demo-chain pitfall: integrate before spending again

A run of demonstrations can be costly without improving the prediction if each new pair is accepted and another request follows without analysis. After each response, record its dimensions, structural lattice/period, output shape, and exact correspondence; then test candidate mappings against all examples received so far. Dissimilar-looking examples may still reveal a common abstraction, so do not treat unfamiliar appearance as a reason to request yet another example.

Before each request, state internally at least two competing rules and the concrete observation expected to distinguish them. If the test rule remains uncertain but the demos already impose no further useful distinction, make a validated prediction from the evidence or acknowledge uncertainty through the protocol's allowed actions; do not reflexively consume the demo cap. A wrong answer should trigger a material reconsideration of scope, output dimensions, or mapping—not a cosmetic variant.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-hermes-luna6-low-seq0/frozen/held-out/play/users/u081/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/continual-arc-demo-integration/references/demo-chain-pitfall.md"}
